	"_comment": "Dansk. Suffixed definite articles: gram.article.definite_suffix is ONE blanket rule per gender for the whole language (fil→filen, problem→problemet; plural filer→filerne via +ne on the authored plural), not a per-noun override — so it only fits consonant-final stems. Danish nouns ending in -e would double up under the blanket 'add en/et' (mappe+en→'mappeen', wrong; real Danish is mappen, -n only): mappe (directory), opgave (task) and pakke (package) are INTENTIONALLY kept as natural vocabulary for done/progress/count/fail composition but are EXCLUDED from definite-suffix reliance — never asserted through DefinitePhrase in the test file. fejl (error, zero plural) and barn→børn (child, irregular plural) are also excluded from the plural-suffix path for the same reason (fejl+ne and børn+ne both misfire). Danish problem→problemer (WITH -er) is a genuine divergence from Swedish problem→problem (invariant) — not a copy-paste leak, checked deliberately. Participles do NOT agree with subject gender in Danish, so no gram.agreement block is declared: 'Fil slettet' and a hypothetical neuter subject would both keep the bare supine. The gerund slot holds the PRESENT TENSE (infinitive+r is exceptionless in Danish) matching the real UI progress convention ('Sletter...'), not a verbal noun. Borrowed dev verbs (pushe, tjekke, committe, merge) follow the standard Danish loanword conjugation (-e infinitive, -et/-ede supine) — committe and merge are the least certain of the set, included on reasonable confidence from casual Danish tech writing.",
